#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""hypr-gamepad: the DualSense drives Hyprland.

The aim: herdr's agents, voice-to-text dictation, and every window, tab, herdr space and workspace,
driven from the controller alone, with the hands off the keyboard.

    hypr-gamepad run [--dry] [--device PATH]   the daemon; hypr-gamepad.service runs it
    hypr-gamepad status                        the pad, its battery, Hyprland
    hypr-gamepad check                         every action builds in the running Hyprland (runs none)
    hypr-gamepad map [--json]                  what each control does, read from LAYOUT

Inside the focused window (herdr here or on another machine, a browser, the Sun board, any window):

    Cross        Enter: send, confirm
    Circle       Esc: back, stop the agent
    Square       take back: delete the word before the cursor (Ctrl+W in herdr and terminals,
                 Ctrl+Backspace elsewhere); held, it keeps deleting, faster and faster. Never Esc:
                 a running agent keeps running
    D-pad        arrow keys (held: they repeat)
    L1 / R1      previous / next tab: herdr's, the browser's, the board's, a window group's
    Left stick   up / down: previous / next agent; left / right: previous / next herdr space
                 (the browser: back / forward; the board: the tile before / after)
    L3           Shift+Tab: the agent's mode (plan, accept edits)
    R3           normal text size in the focused window (Ctrl+0), in either layer
    Right stick  scroll, smoothly: the further it tilts, the faster

Agents that wait on you:

    Triangle     show in herdr the agent that waits on you most; again: the next one
    PS tap       raise the Sun board
    PS held      the sessions wheel on this screen: the right stick lights a slot, let go of PS to
                 show it in herdr; Circle closes it

The map, in either layer:

    Options held what each control does in the focused window, on screen; a tap keeps it up until
                 the next one (the shell plugin ormus.hypr-gamepad-map)

Talk, in either layer:

    R2 held      talk; let go and the words land in the focused window (voice-flow, as Super+H)
    R2 tap       keeps listening until the next tap
    Mic button   talk: a press starts, the next press pastes (needs the udev rule below)
    Circle       while talking: drop the recording, nothing is pasted
    Create       the voice-ideas listener on / off (as Super+Shift+H)

The pad's lights (needs the udev rule below):

    lightbar     gold at the desk, flare red while talking, cream while L2 holds the desktop side,
                 blue in game mode
    player light which screen has focus, as a PS5 numbers its players: one, two, three, four

The udev rule (udev/60-hypr-gamepad.rules, installed by hand into /etc/udev/rules.d) lets the user
at the seat set the lights and read the raw reports, where the mic button is. Without it the pad
still works, without lights and mic button; `hypr-gamepad status` says.

Games:

    PS + Options game mode on / off: the daemon lets go of the pad, so a game reads it; nothing else
                 acts until the same chord brings it back (a long rumble going, a double coming back)

L2 held is the world layer, the desktop:

    D-pad        focus the window in that direction, across screens
    L1 / R1      previous / next workspace on this screen
    Left stick   focus the screen in that direction; with none beside, left / right go to the
                 screen before / after (screens stacked in a column have none beside)
    Right stick  up / down: bigger / smaller text in the focused window (Ctrl+= / Ctrl+-: foot and
                 the herdr windows, browsers); held, it keeps going. A tilt held from scrolling
                 when L2 goes down waits for the stick to come back first
    Cross        full screen on / off
    Circle       back to the workspace before
    Square       next window on this workspace
    Triangle     scratchpad on / off

The daemon grabs the pad, so no other program reads it while it runs; game mode (PS + Options)
gives it to a game. Each press becomes one action from LAYOUT, sent to Hyprland over its request socket as
a Lua dispatcher: 0.5 ms a call, where hyprctl takes 16 ms (measured on a test machine, 2026-10-29). Nothing is
typed into the desktop through uinput: a key for a window goes to that window through Hyprland's
send_shortcut, which hands the window the key and gives focus back in the same call. --dry prints each action instead of sending it, and reads
Hyprland only to look.
"""
